Anal Fistula
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Chronic purulent perianal discharge, Recurrent perianal abscesses, Visible external perianal opening, Palpable fibrous tract

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-004
Reason for Visit: New-onset localized severe pain and swelling with foul-smelling perianal discharge following a history of intermittent drainage.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient only.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified symptoms highly suspicious for a blocked anal fistula progressing to a recurrent perianal abscess. The acute escalation of localized pain and swelling warranted immediate clinical examination to confirm the diagnosis, evaluate for systemic sepsis, and facilitate surgical referral before the onset of necrotizing soft tissue infection, satisfying after-hours criteria.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My bottom is constantly leaking this foul-smelling fluid, and now it's swollen and hurts too much to sit."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 3-month history of chronic, intermittent purulent and blood-stained discharge from a small perianal opening. Reports a history of a spontaneously drained perianal abscess 6 months ago. Over the last 48 hours, the discharge decreased but localized pain, swelling, and redness dramatically increased. Occasional passage of flatus through the external opening noted.
Functional Status: Ambulatory but in significant discomfort; standing or lying on side preferred.
Social/Safety Concerns: Hygiene management causing significant distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 90 | Temp: 37.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Patient uncomfortable, appearing frustrated by chronic symptoms.
Physical Exam: Perineal inspection reveals a visible external opening (secondary tract) 2 cm from the anal verge at the 4 o'clock position in the lithotomy position. A palpable indurated fibrous cord extends from the external opening toward the anal canal. Surrounding mild erythema and localized tenderness, with purulent exudate expressed upon gentle pressure. Exam confirms chronic purulent perianal discharge, recurrent perianal abscess history, visible external perianal opening, and palpable fibrous tract.
Environmental Observations: Clean environment. Sanitary pads being used to manage discharge.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications. Utilizing over-the-counter paracetamol.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Anal Fistula - Status: Exacerbated (Mild recurrent abscess formation)
Differentials:

Hidradenitis Suppurativa - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of multiple interconnected sinus tracts, comedones in other intertriginous areas (axillae, groins), and direct connection to the anal canal.

Pilonidal Sinus - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Location near the anal verge rather than in the natal cleft/sacrococcygeal region.

Prolapsed Internal Hemorrhoid - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of a discrete external opening with purulent discharge and a palpable fibrous tract, rather than mucosal tissue prolapsing from the anal orifice.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute exacerbation of pain and swelling in the context of a known chronic perianal discharge raised immediate clinical concern for an obstructed anal fistula leading to an acute abscess. Urgent out-of-hours physical examination was strictly necessary to determine if surgical incision and drainage were emergently required. While frank fluctuance necessitating immediate ED transfer for surgery was not yet present, the induration, low-grade temperature, and extreme discomfort required immediate initiation of broad-spectrum oral antibiotics and formal general surgical referral, safely preventing rapid progression to severe sepsis overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Amoxicillin/Clavulanate 875/125mg BD for 5 days. Issued an urgent referral to General Surgery for definitive mapping (e.g., MRI pelvis) and surgical management (fistulotomy or seton placement).
Positive Behavioral Change: Place a small, folded, unmedicated gauze pad directly over the external opening and change it every 4 hours. This absorbs the corrosive purulent discharge, preventing severe perianal skin breakdown and intense itching.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Must see regular GP within 48 hours to expedite surgical referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the swelling rapidly increases in size, if you develop a fever greater than 38.5°C, or if you become unable to pass urine, present immediately to the nearest Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Antibiotics will manage the current mild infection, but they will not cure the fistula. Surgery is the only definitive treatment.
Clinical Course: The pain should stabilize as the antibiotics take effect, but the tract will likely continue to drain until surgically addressed.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor the area closely. If the redness spreads rapidly, the pain becomes unbearable, or you feel hot and shivery, go straight to the Emergency Department as this indicates a severe spreading infection.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A fistula is an abnormal tunnel between your bowel and your skin. It has become temporarily blocked, causing an infection and pain. The antibiotics will treat the tissue infection, but a surgeon needs to formally close the tunnel.
Actionable Steps: Take the antibiotics strictly with food to minimize nausea. Ensure you complete the entire course, even if the pain subsides, to fully eradicate the soft tissue infection and reduce the size of the inflamed tract before your surgical consult.

